==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|tally-ho}}

===Etymology===
1772, UK, probably alteration of {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|taïaut|pos=interjection used in deer-hunting}},<ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster Online}}</ref> from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|tahou}}, {{m|frm|tayo}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|taho}}, {{m|fro|ta ho}}, {{m|fro|tielau|pos=interjection given to hounds to return}} (second half of 13th century), composed of {{m|fro|ta|pos=particle used to prod animals}} + {{m|fro|ho|ho !|halt!, hold!}}.<ref>[http://www.cnrtl.fr/etymologie/ta%C3%AFaut Taïaut], ''[http://www.cnrtl.fr/ Centre National de Ressources Textuelles et Lexicales]''</ref> More at {{l|en|ho}}.

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{label|en|UK}} used to [[urge]] on a [[fox hunt]], especially when the [[fox]] is [[sight]]ed.
#: ''There he is! Coming out of that coppice! '''Tallyho tallyho tallyho'''!''
# a simple greeting, exclusively used by the [[upper class]]es.
#: '''''Tallyho''' chaps! What's the time, anybody?''
# {{label|en|radio|aviation}} target sighted.
#: ''(Air Traffic Control): Speedbird 123, New York, traffic at two o’clock, seven miles, a Boeing 737, west-bound, at 4000 feet.”
#: ''(Pilot): New York, Speedbird 123, '''tallyho'''.''

====Usage notes====
In aviation radio usage, typically abbreviated to {{m|en|tally}}. In civilian aviation usage, the official term for “traffic sighted” is “traffic in sight”.<ref>Federal Aviation Administration: [https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/pcg/index.htm Pilot/Controller Glossary (P/CG)], [https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/pcg/T.HTM T] (Traffic)</ref>

